From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: OpenWrt <noreply@openwrt.org>
Date: Fri, 01 Aug 2026 12:00:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] netfilter + airoha: gen-gate PPE FDB flush and
 teardown_by_eth

A WiFi STA that disconnects and reconnects in quick succession loses
hardware offload on the new flow F' because the disconnect's
deferred cleanup work tears down flows by MAC match without checking
the cleanup_gen chronometer that 990-01 introduced for this exact
race:

   T=0      STA disconnects; mac80211 emits SWITCHDEV_FDB_DEL_TO_DEVICE
            990-01 bumps flow_mac_cleanup_gen 0->1 and schedules
            nf_flow_table_mac_cleanup_work(gen=1).
            930 schedules airoha_ppe_fdb_work_handler on eth->fdb_wq.
            0014 (mt76) and 0013 (mt7996) call
            airoha_ppe_dev_flush_flows_by_mac() which schedules MORE
            work on eth->fdb_wq.
   T=eps    STA reconnects; new flow F' created.
            flow_offload_add stamps F'->cleanup_gen = atomic_read(...)=1.
   T=cleanup
            990-01 nf flowtable work: skips F' (cleanup_gen (1) >= gen
            (1)); F' SW/HW state preserved.
            Airoha fdb_work_handler A:
              airoha_ppe_flush_l2_flows_by_mac() walks l2_flows and
                unconditionally removes any L2 master whose L2 MAC
                matches.
              airoha_ppe_flush_l4_flows_by_mac() walks foe_flow[]
                and unconditionally removes any L4 entry whose L2
                rewrite MAC matches -- including F', even though F'
                was committed AFTER the FDB DEL event.
              Calls flow_offload_teardown_by_eth(addr) which runs
              nf_flow_teardown_if_eth() across all flowtables with
              NO cleanup_gen check, calling flow_offload_teardown(F')
              and clearing IPS_OFFLOAD_BIT.
            F' is now in NF_FLOW_TEARDOWN.  Packets fall back to slow
            path; a fresh offload can only start after GC evicts F',
            which can take >1s, and stale conntrack state may block
            re-offload longer.

990-01 already proved the gen-gate pattern: any flow created at or
after the gen captured by the disconnect's bump must be left alone
because it is by construction NOT the stale flow the disconnect
cleanup is meant to drain.  This patch extends the same gate to:

1. The nf_flow_table side: a new flow_offload_teardown_by_eth_gen()
   variant that takes a u32 gen and skips DIRECT flows whose
   cleanup_gen is at or after that gen.  flow_offload_teardown_by_eth()
   becomes a wrapper passing gen=0 (no gate) so external callers
   that drive teardown from outside the switchdev pipeline (debugfs,
   future vendor modules) keep doing the broad teardown.

2. The Airoha PPE side: struct airoha_flow_table_entry grows a u32
   cleanup_gen field (mirroring the existing field on struct
   flow_offload since 990-01).  airoha_ppe_foe_flow_commit_entry()
   and airoha_ppe_foe_l2_flow_commit_entry() stamp
   atomic_read(&flow_mac_cleanup_gen) at commit time, mirroring
   flow_offload_add()'s stamp on the SW flow side.

3. The Airoha PPE deferred worker
   (airoha_ppe_fdb_work_handler) reads flow_mac_cleanup_gen at
   worker-run time (not at schedule time).  The captured gen is
   passed to both flush walks (so PPE entries whose cleanup_gen >=
   gen are skipped) and to flow_offload_teardown_by_eth_gen() (so
   SW flows matching the MAC but stamped at/after this event's gen
   are not torn down).

Both the L2 walk (airoha_ppe_flush_l2_flows_by_mac) and the L4
walk (airoha_ppe_flush_l4_flows_by_mac) get a u32 gen parameter
under #ifdef CONFIG_NET_SWITCHDEV, so the same gate that protects
SW flows in 990-01 now protects the vendor PPE SW state and its
HW FOE binds.

The global atomic_t flow_mac_cleanup_gen is moved from
static-in-nf_flow_table_core.c to a CONFIG_NET_SWITCHDEV-guarded
extern declared in <net/netfilter/nf_flow_table.h> so the Airoha
driver can read it; existing references (the bump in the switchdev
notifier, the stamp in flow_offload_add) remain inside
IS_ENABLED(CONFIG_NET_SWITCHDEV) blocks so non-switchdev builds are
unaffected.

Why capture-at-worker rather than capture-at-schedule:

  airoha_ppe_switchdev_event (patch 930) and 990-01's
  nf_flow_table_switchdev_event are two independent notifier_block
  registrations on the same SWITCHDEV_FDB_DEL_TO_DEVICE event.
  The kernel does not order notifier chains between unrelated
  registrations, so a gen captured at schedule time may read
  pre-bump (gen=0), silently disabling the gate (gen=0 means "no
  gate" in the iterators).  By reading the gen in the deferred
  worker instead, we are guaranteed that 990-01's notifier has
  already run and bumped the counter: notifier chains are executed
  synchronously in process/skb context, and the worker is
  out-of-line work that runs strictly later on eth->fdb_wq.

  The trade-off is over-gating: if several STAs disconnect in
  quick succession (each bumping gen), only the latest bump is
  visible to earlier events' workers.  In practice this is
  harmless -- any flow still alive at the latest gen is by
  definition stamped with that gen and would be skipped by the
  gate anyway.  Per-event granularity is lost; correctness is
  preserved.

The mt76 patch-a (0014) path, the mt7996 0013 path, and the airoha
switchdev notifier in 930 all funnel through
airoha_ppe_schedule_flush_by_mac() into the same deferred worker,
so they all inherit the gen-gate at worker-run time automatically.

This patch does NOT close the smaller TOCTOU window in
flow_offload_work_add() (the patched NF_FLOW_TEARDOWN check at line
981 takes a snapshot, then commits the flow; teardown may occur
between check and commit).  That window is secondary and
self-healing: a subsequent FLOW_CLS_DESTROY tears down the dying
flow shortly after, and the brief HW commit is in correct L2
state so no mis-forwarding occurs.

Signed-off-by: OpenWrt <noreply@openwrt.org>
---

--- a/include/net/netfilter/nf_flow_table.h
+++ b/include/net/netfilter/nf_flow_table.h
@@ -320,6 +320,11 @@
 
 void flow_offload_teardown(struct flow_offload *flow);
 void flow_offload_teardown_by_eth(const u8 *addr);
+void flow_offload_teardown_by_eth_gen(const u8 *addr, u32 gen);
+
+#ifdef CONFIG_NET_SWITCHDEV
+extern atomic_t flow_mac_cleanup_gen;
+#endif
 
 int nf_flow_table_iterate(struct nf_flowtable *flow_table,
                       void (*iter)(struct nf_flowtable *flowtable,
--- a/net/netfilter/nf_flow_table_core.c
+++ b/net/netfilter/nf_flow_table_core.c
@@ -32,7 +32,10 @@
 };
 #endif
 
-static atomic_t flow_mac_cleanup_gen;
+#ifdef CONFIG_NET_SWITCHDEV
+atomic_t flow_mac_cleanup_gen;
+EXPORT_SYMBOL_GPL(flow_mac_cleanup_gen);
+#endif
 
 static RAW_NOTIFIER_HEAD(flow_teardown_by_eth_chain);
 
@@ -419,12 +422,29 @@
 }
 EXPORT_SYMBOL_GPL(flow_offload_teardown);
 
-static void nf_flow_teardown_if_eth(struct nf_flowtable *flow_table,
-				    struct flow_offload *flow, void *data)
+struct nf_flow_teardown_by_eth_ctx {
+	const u8 *addr;
+	u32 gen;
+};
+
+static void nf_flow_teardown_if_eth_gen(struct nf_flowtable *flow_table,
+					struct flow_offload *flow, void *data)
 {
-	const u8 *addr = data;
+	const struct nf_flow_teardown_by_eth_ctx *ctx = data;
+	const u8 *addr = ctx->addr;
 	int dir;
 
+	/* Skip flows created at or after the generation that triggered
+	 * this teardown.  They are fresh flows for the same MAC that
+	 * were established AFTER the disconnect event whose address
+	 * prompted this teardown (e.g. PPE L2 cache drain after a
+	 * WiFi STA disconnect), so clobbering them would re-introduce
+	 * the very stall that the disconnect-teardown path exists to
+	 * fix.  See 990-01 for the original cleanup_gen design.
+	 */
+	if (ctx->gen && flow->cleanup_gen >= ctx->gen)
+		return;
+
 	for (dir = 0; dir < FLOW_OFFLOAD_DIR_MAX; dir++) {
 		struct flow_offload_tuple *t = &flow->tuplehash[dir].tuple;
 
@@ -442,6 +462,13 @@
 
 void flow_offload_teardown_by_eth(const u8 *addr)
 {
+	flow_offload_teardown_by_eth_gen(addr, 0);
+}
+EXPORT_SYMBOL_GPL(flow_offload_teardown_by_eth);
+
+void flow_offload_teardown_by_eth_gen(const u8 *addr, u32 gen)
+{
+	struct nf_flow_teardown_by_eth_ctx ctx = { .addr = addr, .gen = gen };
 	struct nf_flowtable *flowtable;
 
 	if (!addr || is_zero_ether_addr(addr) ||
@@ -459,11 +486,11 @@
 
 	mutex_lock(&flowtable_lock);
 	list_for_each_entry(flowtable, &flowtables, list)
-		nf_flow_table_iterate(flowtable, nf_flow_teardown_if_eth,
-				      (void *)addr);
+		nf_flow_table_iterate(flowtable, nf_flow_teardown_if_eth_gen,
+				      &ctx);
 	mutex_unlock(&flowtable_lock);
 }
-EXPORT_SYMBOL_GPL(flow_offload_teardown_by_eth);
+EXPORT_SYMBOL_GPL(flow_offload_teardown_by_eth_gen);
 
 int nf_flow_offload_teardown_by_eth_register_notifier(struct notifier_block *nb)
 {
--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/airoha/airoha_eth.h
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/airoha/airoha_eth.h
@@ -504,6 +504,22 @@
 	struct hlist_node l2_subflow_node; /* PPE L2 subflow entry */
 	u32 hash;
 
+#ifdef CONFIG_NET_SWITCHDEV
+	/* Generation at the time this flow was committed to the Airoha
+	 * PPE driver SW cache (foe_flow[] or l2_flows).  Pairs with the
+	 * global flow_mac_cleanup_gen from nf_flow_table_core.c and the
+	 * gen captured by airoha_ppe_fdb_work when it is queued from a
+	 * switchdev FDB event or a WiFi STA-removal invocation.  The
+	 * flush-by-MAC walks in 930 (airoha_ppe_flush_l2_flows_by_mac /
+	 * airoha_ppe_flush_l4_flows_by_mac) and the
+	 * flow_offload_teardown_by_eth_gen() flow-table walk all skip
+	 * entries whose cleanup_gen is at or after the captured gen,
+	 * so a NEW flow created after the disconnect event is NOT
+	 * torn down by the delayed cleanup of the disconnect.
+	 */
+	u32 cleanup_gen;
+#endif
+
 	struct airoha_foe_stats64 stats;
 	enum airoha_flow_entry_type type;
 
--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/airoha/airoha_ppe.c
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/airoha/airoha_ppe.c
@@ -1119,6 +1119,9 @@
 	struct airoha_flow_table_entry *prev;
 
 	e->type = FLOW_TYPE_L2;
+#ifdef CONFIG_NET_SWITCHDEV
+	e->cleanup_gen = atomic_read(&flow_mac_cleanup_gen);
+#endif
 	prev = rhashtable_lookup_get_insert_fast(&ppe->l2_flows, &e->l2_node,
 						 airoha_l2_flow_table_params);
 	if (!prev)
@@ -1144,6 +1147,9 @@
 	hash = airoha_ppe_foe_get_entry_hash(ppe, &e->data);
 	e->type = FLOW_TYPE_L4;
 	e->hash = 0xffff;
+#ifdef CONFIG_NET_SWITCHDEV
+	e->cleanup_gen = atomic_read(&flow_mac_cleanup_gen);
+#endif
 
 	spin_lock_bh(&ppe_lock);
 	hlist_add_head(&e->list, &ppe->foe_flow[hash]);
@@ -1512,7 +1518,11 @@
 }
 
 static bool airoha_ppe_flush_l2_flows_by_mac(struct airoha_eth *eth,
-					     const u8 *addr)
+					     const u8 *addr
+#ifdef CONFIG_NET_SWITCHDEV
+					     , u32 gen
+#endif
+					     )
 {
 	struct airoha_ppe *ppe = eth->ppe;
 	struct airoha_flow_table_entry *e;
@@ -1524,8 +1534,21 @@
 	rhashtable_walk_enter(&ppe->l2_flows, &iter);
 	rhashtable_walk_start(&iter);
 	while ((e = rhashtable_walk_next(&iter)) != NULL) {
-		if (!IS_ERR(e) && airoha_ppe_l2_flow_has_mac(e, addr))
-			hlist_add_head(&e->l2_subflow_node, &to_remove);
+		if (IS_ERR(e))
+			continue;
+		if (!airoha_ppe_l2_flow_has_mac(e, addr))
+			continue;
+#ifdef CONFIG_NET_SWITCHDEV
+		/* Skip L2 master flows created at or after this event's
+		 * captured generation -- they are NEW flows for the same
+		 * MAC established AFTER the disconnect that triggered
+		 * this flush, so removing them would re-introduce the
+		 * very stall we are trying to recover from.
+		 */
+		if (gen && e->cleanup_gen >= gen)
+			continue;
+#endif
+		hlist_add_head(&e->l2_subflow_node, &to_remove);
 	}
 	rhashtable_walk_stop(&iter);
 	rhashtable_walk_exit(&iter);
@@ -1552,7 +1575,11 @@
 }
 
 static bool airoha_ppe_flush_l4_flows_by_mac(struct airoha_ppe *ppe,
-					     const u8 *addr)
+					     const u8 *addr
+#ifdef CONFIG_NET_SWITCHDEV
+					     , u32 gen
+#endif
+					     )
 {
 	u32 sram_num_entries = airoha_ppe_get_total_sram_num_entries(ppe);
 	struct airoha_flow_table_entry *e;
@@ -1563,9 +1590,19 @@
 	spin_lock_bh(&ppe_lock);
 	for (i = 0; i < sram_num_entries; i++) {
 		hlist_for_each_entry(e, &ppe->foe_flow[i], list) {
-			if (e->type == FLOW_TYPE_L4 &&
-			    airoha_ppe_l4_flow_has_mac(e, addr))
-				hlist_add_head(&e->l2_subflow_node, &to_remove);
+			if (e->type != FLOW_TYPE_L4)
+				continue;
+			if (!airoha_ppe_l4_flow_has_mac(e, addr))
+				continue;
+#ifdef CONFIG_NET_SWITCHDEV
+			/* Skip L4 flows created at or after this event's
+			 * captured generation; see comment in
+			 * airoha_ppe_flush_l2_flows_by_mac().
+			 */
+			if (gen && e->cleanup_gen >= gen)
+				continue;
+#endif
+			hlist_add_head(&e->l2_subflow_node, &to_remove);
 		}
 	}
 	spin_unlock_bh(&ppe_lock);
@@ -1590,36 +1627,99 @@
 {
 	struct airoha_ppe_fdb_work *w = container_of(work, struct airoha_ppe_fdb_work,
 						     work);
+#ifdef CONFIG_NET_SWITCHDEV
+	/* Read flow_mac_cleanup_gen here, in the deferred worker, rather
+	 * than capturing it in airoha_ppe_schedule_flush_by_mac() at
+	 * schedule time.  At schedule time there is no guaranteed
+	 * ordering between this driver's switchdev notifier
+	 * (airoha_ppe_switchdev_event, patch 930) and 990-01's
+	 * nf_flow_table_switchdev_event -- they are two independent
+	 * notifier_block registrations on the same switchdev event,
+	 * so a gen captured at schedule time could read pre-bump (0)
+	 * and the gate would silently disable itself (gen=0 means
+	 * "no gate" in the iterator).
+	 *
+	 * At worker-run time, by contrast, both notifiers have long
+	 * since completed (notifier chains run synchronously in
+	 * process/skb context; deferred work runs later out-of-line
+	 * on eth->fdb_wq).  So any 990-01 bump that was going to
+	 * happen for the triggering FDB DEL has already happened, and
+	 * the read here returns a value that any flow F' created
+	 * *after* the disconnect already stamped into its
+	 * flow_offload->cleanup_gen via 990-01's
+	 * flow_offload_add() path.
+	 *
+	 * The cost of reading-at-worker instead of capturing-at-
+	 * schedule is over-gating: if several STAs disconnect in
+	 * succession (each bumping gen), the worker for the earliest
+	 * event sees the latest (highest) gen and may skip flows that
+	 * the earlier event alone would have torn down.  In practice
+	 * this is harmless -- any flow still alive at the latest gen
+	 * is by definition at that gen and would have been skipped by
+	 * the gate anyway.
+	 */
+	u32 gen = atomic_read(&flow_mac_cleanup_gen);
+	void (*teardown_by_eth_gen)(const u8 *addr, u32 gen);
+#else
 	void (*teardown_by_eth)(const u8 *addr);
+#endif
 	bool l4_removed;
 	bool l2_removed;
 
 	mutex_lock(&flow_offload_mutex);
 	if (rcu_access_pointer(w->eth->npu)) {
-		l2_removed = airoha_ppe_flush_l2_flows_by_mac(w->eth, w->addr);
+		l2_removed = airoha_ppe_flush_l2_flows_by_mac(w->eth, w->addr
+#ifdef CONFIG_NET_SWITCHDEV
+							      , gen
+#endif
+							      );
 		l4_removed = airoha_ppe_flush_l4_flows_by_mac(w->eth->ppe,
-							      w->addr);
+							      w->addr
+#ifdef CONFIG_NET_SWITCHDEV
+							      , gen
+#endif
+							      );
 		mutex_unlock(&flow_offload_mutex);
 
 		if (l2_removed || l4_removed) {
-			teardown_by_eth = symbol_get(flow_offload_teardown_by_eth);
-			if (teardown_by_eth) {
+#ifdef CONFIG_NET_SWITCHDEV
+			teardown_by_eth_gen = symbol_get(flow_offload_teardown_by_eth_gen);
+			if (teardown_by_eth_gen) {
 				/* Set the in-flight guard so our own
 				 * airoha_ppe_teardown_by_eth_notifier()
 				 * (invoked synchronously by
-				 * flow_offload_teardown_by_eth()) does
+				 * flow_offload_teardown_by_eth_gen()) does
 				 * not queue another round of L2/L4
 				 * flush work for the same MAC we
 				 * just finished draining.  Redundant
 				 * work on the single-threaded
 				 * eth->fdb_wq would also re-acquire
 				 * flow_offload_mutex twice per event.
+				 *
+				 * Pass the gen captured above so the
+				 * gen-aware flowtable walk skips SW
+				 * flows that were created after the
+				 * FDB/STA event (e.g. a flow F'
+				 * created by a STA reconnecting
+				 * before this delayed handler
+				 * finished), matching the
+				 * cleanup_gen-aware behaviour of the
+				 * original 990-01 path.
 				 */
 				w->eth->teardown_in_flight = true;
+				teardown_by_eth_gen(w->addr, gen);
+				w->eth->teardown_in_flight = false;
+				symbol_put(flow_offload_teardown_by_eth_gen);
+			}
+#else
